## WHAT THE API SENDS (FACT 3 holds the pointers; the cases below are the property the cells pin)
| Case | What the Api sends | The unpriced document prints |
|---|---|---|
| code present | `code: "<text>"`, text not blank | `<code> <description>` (D3) |
| code absent | no `code` member (the schema makes it optional) | exactly today's unpriced text for that line |
| code null | `code: null` (the schema allows it) | as absent |
| code empty | `code: ""` (the Engine writes this when the catalogue row is missing) | as absent |
| code blank | spaces only (a manual item's code is free text of 1 to 60 characters) | as absent |
| code equals description | the same trimmed string (a manual item can be entered that way) | the description once |
| digits and a dot | `code: "4521.10"`, which the CURRENT check reads as an amount (FACT 8) | printed as data; the check is position-aware (D5) |
| a code that equals a real amount string | a manual item whose code text equals a package total | printed as data; a leak of the same amount ELSEWHERE is still found |
The first lane's fixtures hold NO row in any case but "code present": every one of the 13 description rows and 2 software rows of `s4Proposal` carries a code, and the 5 lines of `fiveClassDealProposal` do (FACT 3). **You build the other cases by code in the test file** (synthetic values; no real code).

## THE ORACLE (D5 and D6: what you change in `web/src/proposal/unpriced.test.tsx`; the lines are base lines)
The oracle at base: `visibleText` (`:75`) strips the `<style>` element and collapses whitespace; `decimalLeaves` (`:84`) collects every string leaf of the proposal that matches `^-?[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$` except the key `taxRate` and the values `0` and `0.00`; `displayForms` (`:99`) gives the value and its `formatMoney` and `formatRate` forms with the symbol and any minus or en dash removed; `figuresIn` (`:104`) reports a hit for `$` in the text and for every form found in the text. Its three weaknesses, two the review showed and one this lane meets:
1. **It reads text only** (follow-up 1): an amount in a `data-*` attribute stays green (plant P1).
2. **It reads only full display forms** (follow-up 1): the whole-dollar fragment `57,940` with no `$` stays green (plant P2), because the forms are `57940.00`, `57,940.00` and `57,940.0000`.
3. **It reads a part code made of digits and a dot as an amount** (this lane; MEASURED, FACT 8): a code `4521.10` is a `decimalLeaves` member (the leaf has the key `code`, which nothing excludes), its form `4521.10` is a substring of the unpriced text once the code is printed, and `figuresIn` reports a hit on a document that holds no price.
**The design (the property first; the cell names are yours):**
- **Position-aware, not allow-listed.** Before the figure scan, take the text and, for each (code, description) pair found by walking the proposal (an object with a non-blank string `code` and a string `description` or `label`), replace each occurrence of the exact string `<code> <description>` by `<description>` (string `split` and `join`; **no `new RegExp(` from a variable**, the first lane's hand-row rule). The codes that remain in the text are the ones printed OUT of position. **Measured with the probe (FACT 8):** the code in position is clean after the strip; a real amount leaked beside it is still found; a code printed a second time outside its position is found; a code that equals a real amount, in position, is clean, and the same amount leaked bare elsewhere or as `4,521.10` is found.
- **Attribute values and whole-dollar forms (follow-up 1).** The scanned text is the stripped visible text plus the value of EVERY attribute of EVERY element of the document (each on its own line; attribute names are not scanned). For each decimal leaf whose fraction is all zeros and whose integer part has 3 or more digits, two more forms: the bare digits (`57940`) and the digits grouped with commas (`57,940`), each matched as a TOKEN (not preceded by a digit, a dot or a comma; not followed by a digit, or by a comma or a dot and a digit), by a small function that walks the text with `indexOf` and tests the neighbouring characters (no `new RegExp(` from a variable). Integer parts of 1 or 2 digits are not scanned as whole-dollar forms (a quantity `7` is not a price); say that in a comment. **UNMEASURED: whether the hardened oracle reports any hit on the unpriced renders of the four fixtures at base** (the drafter cannot render): Part 1 prints the hits; the expected number is 0 for every combination; a hit is classified by hand (a real figure is a product defect: STOP with the site; a collision of the token with data is argued and, if real, the token rule is narrowed with a stated reason) and never silenced.
- **U10 narrowed (follow-up 2).** At base U10 accepts an unpriced leaf that is "the text of, or a part of, a leaf of the priced render" (`:244`); a phrase made of existing words (`package price`, the planted Qty cell) passes. New rule: with the part codes removed by the same position strip, every leaf of the unpriced render is exactly the text of a leaf of the priced render, **except** a leaf equal to `K.NO_MONTHLY_TOTAL` (`template/concise.ts:46`), which must be the last sentence of some priced leaf. That one named exception is the short form's rate paragraph (`OnePager.tsx:347-350`: the priced paragraph holds `T.rateMethod(...)`, a space and `K.NO_MONTHLY_TOTAL`; the unpriced one holds the last only). The exception list is a constant of ONE entry and a cell asserts its length is 1. **If the narrowed U10 is red at base on any leaf that is not that one: STOP with the leaf and the document (it is a second shape the first lane's refinement admitted); do not widen the exception.**
- **The oracle can fail (controls, no source plant needed):** cells over small synthetic DOM that assert the oracle FINDS an amount in a `data-total` attribute, in an `aria-label`, in a `title`; FINDS `57,940` and `57940` as tokens; does NOT find `157,940` for the figure `57940.00`; does not scan a 2-digit integer part; and, for the position strip, the five rows (A to G) of FACT 8.

