## Questions with defaults (Tuesday answers at the stamp; every default is already assumed above)
- Q1 (D1, the sites): default = every site that prints a line item's description from a row that carries a `code` (`L1` to `L6`). Alternative: package detail lines only (`L1`, `L2`, `L5`), leaving the optional items and the software rows as they are (the card's detail says "before each description").
- Q2 (D2, a code equal to the description): default = the description once. Alternative: always both (reads as a repeated word).
- Q3 (D3, the separator): default = one space. Alternative: none; any visible separator is a new character in a customer document and needs the product owner.
- Q4 (D2, a blank code): default = trimmed, and a blank one is "none". Alternative: print as sent (leading and trailing spaces collapse in a page anyway).
- Q5 (D6, size): default = ONE lane, size M, both follow-ups folded in. Alternative: split into Part 1 (oracle, narrowed U10, goldens; no source change; G2 gate) and Part 2 (the codes; G1 gate), serial, same seat or two.
- Q6 (goldens): default = eight files if they total 1.5 MiB or less, else STOP; alternative eight digests (loses the diff).
- Q7 (gate): G1 on Opus. If Tuesday reads it as tests-and-UI only, G2; the build is the same.
- Q8 (builder level): default `sonnet` per routing row C2. `brief_seat.sh:33` now also accepts `fable`, and a comment above it records an instruction of 2026-11-19 that every Datasec agent run on the newest Fable: Tuesday decides the level at the stamp (the model id is read from `models_latest.json`, never typed).
- Q9 (the ticket): default = no ticket of its own; the commit names MPSCALC-278. Alternative: a records seat files a follow-up key first and the subject names it.
- Q10 (the clock): the card's ruling stamp reads 12:52:39 and the commission names the tap as 12:52:16; Tuesday decides which clock the stamp quotes. This kit quotes neither as a tap time.
- Q11 (the gate's PDF row): default = vitest only in the lane; the gate prints both unpriced documents to PDF in Chrome and reads them with `pdftotext`, comparing page counts with the first lane's (RELAYED: full 13 pages, short form 3 pages) because a code lengthens cells (UNMEASURED).
- Q12 (the whole-dollar rule): default = 3 or more integer digits, token-bounded. Alternative: all integer parts (false hits on every quantity).
- Q13 (counts): the new cells' totals and the golden sizes are UNMEASURED by the drafter; you print them.

